      "writtenOn": "2026-04-23T10:09:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e BTW I tried removing those 2 lines in the Makefile added by this patch, and it still compiled and worked just fine.\n\nIndeed when building within android env I don\u0027t see this issue but if build outside of android I am getting below errors without __NO_FORTIFY\n\n```\nld: vmlinux.o: in function `fortify_memcpy_chk\u0027:\n..common/staging/./include/linux/fortify-string.h:599:(.pkvm.text+0xcce0): undefined reference to `__fortify_panic__pkvm\u0027\nld: ../include/linux/fortify-string.h:599:(.pkvm.text+0xccf2): undefined reference to `__fortify_panic__pkvm\u0027\nld: vmlinux.o: in function `__fortify_strlen\u0027:\n../include/linux/fortify-string.h:271:(.pkvm.text+0xcd06): undefined reference to `__fortify_panic__pkvm\u0027\nld: vmlinux.o: in function `strnlen\u0027:\n../include/linux/fortify-string.h:235:(.pkvm.text+0xcd19): undefined reference to `__fortify_panic__pkvm\u0027\nmake[2]: *** [scripts/Makefile.vmlinux:72: vmlinux.unstripped] Error 1\nmake[1]: *** [../common/staging/Makefile:1271: vmlinux] Error 2\nmake: *** [Makefile:266: __sub-make] Error\n```\n\nSeems like the issue is seen only with GCC and not with Clang/LLVM and is probably due to different optimization strategy done by those toolchains.\n\n\u003e This is not the first time we use memcpy in the hypervisor code. For example, we use it in multiple places in arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c. So why do we need __NO_FORTIFY here if we don\u0027t need it there?\n\nGood question - I am not sure but assume that this is probably due to GCC optimization and memcpy on the other places are probably simple enough to verify on compile time: in those other hyp code I see it is mostly used with `ARRAY_SIZE` and `sizeof` which is known at compile time.",
      "parentUuid": "592e2b14_177a339f",
      "revId": "1d0c65d4747120a9127243d4fd4583ea9fc2f8e6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
